  "enhanced_information": "Osteoblastic osteosarcoma, particularly in the distal femur, typically requires a multimodal treatment approach. The standard protocol involves neoadjuvant chemotherapy to shrink the tumor, followed by surgical resection of the tumor, and then adjuvant chemotherapy to reduce the risk of recurrence. Amputation is less common due to advances in limb salvage techniques. The reasoning chains consistently support this treatment strategy, emphasizing the importance of neoadjuvant chemotherapy and subsequent surgery. Therefore, the most appropriate treatment protocol is neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by surgery and adjuvant chemotherapy.",
  "analysis": "Osteosarcoma, particularly in the distal femur, is typically treated with a multimodal approach. The standard treatment protocol involves neoadjuvant chemotherapy to reduce the size of the tumor before surgical intervention. This is followed by surgical resection of the tumor, which may involve limb-salvage techniques rather than amputation, depending on the case. After surgery, adjuvant chemotherapy is administered to minimize the risk of recurrence. Radiation therapy is not a standard treatment for osteosarcoma, as these tumors are generally resistant to radiation. Therefore, the most appropriate treatment protocol for this case is neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by surgery and adjuvant chemotherapy, which aligns with the enhanced information provided. Thus, the best-supported option is opb.",
